          "heading": "Japan's new kick-catch plan is being countered by opponents, analysis argues",
          "body": "The note.com analysis says Japan have used a new kick-catch method since the start of this year, with player B rather than player A making the catch. The writer says it reduces the risk of penalties for escorting, but the catcher must run backwards and gets support only from player A. The analysis says the method is now being countered, and suggests opponents could let Japan catch and then apply pressure, or drop kicks deeper. It says the issue was highlighted in the Fiji match."
        },
